{"asks": "Whether the trial court will suppress cocaine found in a car search after a driver was arrested for driving on a suspended license and secured in a squad car. The choice is between A (suppress as an unreasonable warrantless search) and E (suppress because no probable cause existed to search the car).", "evidence_quote": "The Court ruled that law-enforcement officers may search automobiles following arrest only if the arrestee \"could have accessed his car at the time of the search.\"", "reasoning": "Under Arizona v. Gant (passage 8), a vehicle search incident to arrest is allowed only if the arrestee could reach the car at the time of the search, or if police have reason to believe the car contains evidence of the offense of arrest. Here the driver was secured in the rear of the squad car, so she could not reach the vehicle. The offense of arrest was driving on a suspended license, which gives no reason to think the car holds evidence of that offense. So the search cannot be justified as incident to arrest. The passages do not establish probable cause for the car, so E's reasoning is not supported by the evidence, though the passages say nothing directly about probable cause for this stop. The conclusion in A, an unreasonable warrantless search leading to suppression, follows from the Gant evidence. The passages do not cover every route to the same result, such as whether the automobile exception could apply, so my confidence is not complete.", "winner": "A", "evidence_decides": true, "confidence": 80}
```

